# What never enters the build context.
#
# Two reasons to keep this tight. The obvious one is speed: the context is
# uploaded to the daemon on every build. The one that actually bites is
# correctness — anything listed here is also invisible to `COPY . .`, so a
# `node_modules` or `dist` left over from a developer's host machine can never
# be baked into an image and silently override what the build just produced.
# See the manifests stage in the Dockerfile for how layer caching survives
# this.

# Installed and built artefacts. The image builds its own from the lockfile.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and coverage output.
**/coverage
**/.vitest

# Tests. The image ships a server, not a test suite; `pnpm test` runs in CI
# against the workspace, not against the image.
**/*.test.ts
tests/
server/tests/

# Version control and CI. The build must not depend on git metadata; anything
# it needs to know about the commit arrives as the VCS_REF build argument.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# Agent and editor state. `.claude/worktrees/` in particular contains entire
# nested checkouts of this repository.
.claude
.idea
.vscode
*.swp
.DS_Store

# Secrets. A `.env` copied into a layer is readable by anyone who pulls the
# image, forever, whatever a later RUN deletes.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Documentation and prose. `LICENSE`, `LICENSE-AGPL` and `LICENSE-MIT` have no
# extension and so survive this rule deliberately: the image ships them.
docs/
*.md

# Local development tooling that the image neither runs nor needs.
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
biome.json
vitest.config.ts
scripts/
.editorconfig
.nvmrc

# Logs.
*.log
